2. ADMISSIBILITY OF THE REQUESTED INPUTS (premise check)
==============================================================================
(a) wall ratio eta = 1.02
    eta = r_inner / r_outer, so eta must lie in [0, 1); eta >= 1 means the inner radius
    exceeds the outer radius -- a shell of negative thickness. It is not a small error:
    there is no microstructure it describes.
    foamsim rejects it: ValueError: eta must be in [0,1), got 1.02
    Also inconsistent with the grade: K46 (true density 0.46 g/cm^3, borosilicate shell
    rho = 2.54 g/cm^3) implies eta = 0.9356, wall t/R = 0.0644.
    'K46' and 'eta = 1.02' are two mutually contradictory specifications of the same particle.

(b) particle volume fraction vf = 0.75
    Random close packing of MONODISPERSE spheres is RCP = 0.64. A monodisperse packing
    cannot exceed it; even the FCC/HCP ordered limit is 0.7405, still below 0.75.
    So 0.75 vol% monodisperse microballoons is geometrically unreachable (a broad
    polydisperse size distribution would be needed, and even then 0.75 is aggressive).
    foamsim rejects it: ValueError: vf=0.75 exceeds random close packing (0.64) of monodisperse spheres

=> 2 blocking problem(s):
   1. eta = 1.02 is impossible (requires 0 <= eta < 1)
   2. vf = 0.75 exceeds monodisperse random close packing (0.64)
=> NO modulus or density is reported at (vf = 0.75, eta = 1.02). The requested state does
   not exist, so any number produced for it would be an artefact of ignoring the physics.

